/-! # Tame radical normalization of a marked curve

Source: de Jong, Families of curves and alterations (1997), §3.5, printed p.607;
Stacks Project, Tags 09I6, 0359, 03PL, 09EB, 0B8Y, 03H0, 0BUG
(blueprint: lem-sfb-tame-radical-curve-normalization).
-/

@[expose] public section

set_option autoImplicit false

universe u

open CategoryTheory CategoryTheory.Limits

namespace AlgebraicGeometry

/-- The normalization in the specified radical field is smooth, with its canonical finite
separable constant field and finite étale marking.
Source: de Jong §3.5, printed p.607; Stacks Tags 09I6, 03PL, 09EB, 0B8Y, 03H0, 0BUG
(blueprint: lem-sfb-tame-radical-curve-normalization).

The equation `hK` fixes the ground-field inclusion to the structure morphism. The scalar
tower fixes the inclusion into Ω. `hL` specifies the actual subfield generated by the chosen
roots in that algebraic closure. `hU` specifies the complement of the branch subscheme.
The two range conditions say that the function and its inverse are regular at each point
of this complement. The generic-point equation fixes the factorization through the actual
relative algebraic closure `algebraicClosure K L`, rather than an arbitrary constant field.
The pullback marking is itself reduced, so it is exactly its reduced inverse image.

Proof plan (the ten steps of the node):
1. lem-sfb-multiradical-galois-degree gives finite Galois degree dividing n^r; thus the
   degree is prime to the positive characteristic, including r = 0.
2. lem-weil-pbc-normalization-in-residue-field-extension and
   lem-fin-normalization-extension-finite give the normal integral finite normalization.
   On nonempty affine charts, lem-sc-integral-closure-dedekind-surjective-fibres gives
   dimension one and surjectivity; use lem-weil-pbc-dense-open-dimension-comparison and
   lem-weil-pbc-finite-type-over-noetherian for the dimension and Noetherian hypotheses.
3. lem-fin-finite-over-projective over Spec K makes the normalization projective, and
   lem-kun-projective-implies-proper makes it proper.
4. Over the complement of D, form the successive unit-root algebras. They are finite étale
   by lem-sfb-unit-binomial-finite-etale and lem-weil-pbc-etale-composition-cancellation.
   lem-trf-smooth-geometrically-normal gives normality of the base, and
   lem-sfb-finite-etale-normal-generic-components identifies the selected field component
   with the normalization. For r = 0 this is the base itself.
5. At D, the residue field is finite separable by
   lem-weil-pbc-etale-algebra-residue-field-separable. The local ring is a DVR by
   lem-sfb-normal-curve-separable-point-smooth and lem-trf-smooth-geometrically-normal.
   lem-sbc-dvr-galois-normalization-ramification-dvd makes the upstairs residue degree
   divide the prime-to-characteristic Galois degree. Minimal-polynomial degrees divide
   this degree, so the derivative criterion implies separability over the residue field
   and hence over K, also in characteristic zero.
6. lem-weil-pbc-integral-prime-over-maximal-maximal makes the points above D closed.
   lem-sfb-normal-curve-separable-point-smooth makes the normalization smooth there.
   Off D use lem-sbc-smooth-relative-dimension-permanence and step 4.
7. lem-sfb-normal-stein-constant-field identifies the global-section field with E inside L
   and constructs the canonical factorization, finite over K with geometrically connected
   fibres. lem-trf-smooth-geometrically-normal gives geometric reducedness;
   lem-pc-proper-geometrically-reduced-global-sections-separable gives E/K separable.
   lem-weil-pbc-finite-separable-base-refinement gives smooth projectivity over E.
8. After every extension of E the curve is connected and smooth. Apply
   lem-weil-pbc-smooth-over-field-regular-local and lem-sc-regular-connected-integral
   to obtain integrality. The canonical generic-point identification preserves L.
9. Base-change finiteness and étaleness to M using lem-trf-finite-radicial-basechange and
   lem-weil-pbc-etale-scheme-base-change. Compose over K, then cancel the finite separable
   E/K map using lem-weil-pbc-finite-separable-extension-etale and
   lem-weil-pbc-etale-composition-cancellation. A finite K-spanning set also spans over E.
   etale-proposition-etale-reduced shows that this pullback is already reduced.
10. Apply lem-sfb-normal-stein-constant-field also to C. Its global sections are K by
    lem-pc-h0-proper-variety and geometric integrality. Thus K is algebraically closed in F;
    elements in the intersection E ∩ F are algebraic over K, so are precisely K. -/
theorem tame_radical_curve_normalization
    (K : Type u) [Field K] (C : Scheme.{u})
    (q : C ⟶ Spec (CommRingCat.of K)) [IsSmoothProjectiveCurve q]
    [GeometricallyIntegral q] [Algebra K C.functionField]
    (hK : algebraMap K C.functionField =
      (C.presheaf.germ ⊤ (genericPoint C) trivial).hom.comp
        (q.appTop.hom.comp (Scheme.ΓSpecIso (CommRingCat.of K)).inv.hom))
    (n : ℕ) (hn : 0 < n) (hnK : IsUnit (n : K))
    (hroots : ∃ ζ : K, IsPrimitiveRoot ζ n)
    (r : ℕ) (a : Fin r → C.functionFieldˣ)
    (D M : Scheme.{u}) (iD : D ⟶ C) (iM : M ⟶ C)
    [IsClosedImmersion iD] [IsClosedImmersion iM] [IsReduced D] [IsReduced M]
    [IsFinite (iD ≫ q)] [Etale (iD ≫ q)]
    [IsFinite (iM ≫ q)] [Etale (iM ≫ q)]
    (hdisj : Disjoint (Set.range iD) (Set.range iM))
    (U : C.Opens) (hU : (U : Set C) = (Set.range iD)ᶜ)
    (hreg : ∀ (j : Fin r) (x : C), x ∈ U →
      (a j : C.functionField) ∈ Set.range (algebraMap (C.presheaf.stalk x) C.functionField) ∧
      (a j : C.functionField)⁻¹ ∈ Set.range (algebraMap (C.presheaf.stalk x) C.functionField))
    (Ω : Type u) [Field Ω] [Algebra C.functionField Ω] [IsAlgClosure C.functionField Ω]
    [Algebra K Ω] [IsScalarTower K C.functionField Ω]
    (α : Fin r → Ω) (hα : ∀ j : Fin r, α j ^ n = algebraMap C.functionField Ω (a j))
    (L : IntermediateField C.functionField Ω)
    (hL : L = IntermediateField.adjoin C.functionField (Set.range α)) :
    FiniteDimensional C.functionField L ∧ IsGalois C.functionField L ∧
    FiniteDimensional K (algebraicClosure K L) ∧
    Algebra.IsSeparable K (algebraicClosure K L) ∧
    IsFinite (C.fromNormalizationIn L) ∧ Surjective (C.fromNormalizationIn L) ∧
    Etale (C.fromNormalizationIn L ∣_ U) ∧ Smooth (C.fromNormalizationIn L ≫ q) ∧
    algebraicClosure K C.functionField = ⊥ ∧
    (∀ z : C.functionField, algebraMap C.functionField L z ∈ algebraicClosure K L ↔
      z ∈ Set.range (algebraMap K C.functionField)) ∧
    (∃ e : Spec (CommRingCat.of L) ≅ Spec (C.normalizationIn L).functionField,
      e.hom ≫ (C.normalizationIn L).fromSpecFunctionField =
        (C.fromSpecOfAlgebraFunctionField L).toNormalization) ∧
    ∃ qE : C.normalizationIn L ⟶ Spec (CommRingCat.of (algebraicClosure K L)),
      qE ≫ Spec.map (CommRingCat.ofHom (algebraMap K (algebraicClosure K L))) =
        C.fromNormalizationIn L ≫ q ∧
      (C.fromSpecOfAlgebraFunctionField L).toNormalization ≫ qE =
        Spec.map (CommRingCat.ofHom (algebraMap (algebraicClosure K L) L)) ∧
      IsSmoothProjectiveCurve qE ∧ GeometricallyIntegral qE ∧
      IsReduced (pullback (C.fromNormalizationIn L) iM) ∧
      IsFinite (pullback.fst (C.fromNormalizationIn L) iM ≫ qE) ∧
      Etale (pullback.fst (C.fromNormalizationIn L) iM ≫ qE) := by
  sorry

end AlgebraicGeometry
